/*!

\page usdProfiles_page_front UsdProfiles : USD Profiles API Schema
\if ( PXRUSDAPI_DOXYGEN_BUILD )
\addtogroup usdProfiles
\endif

__UsdProfiles__ implements the
<a href="https://github.com/PixarAnimationStudios/OpenUSD-proposals/tree/main/proposals/profiles">USD-Profiles proposal</a>,
providing a declarative capability taxonomy for OpenUSD. It enables
applications and assets to declare, discover, and validate functional
capabilities through profiles and a capability directed acyclic graph (DAG).

\tableofcontents

\section usdProfiles_purpose Purpose and Motivation

USD's flexibility via custom schemas, proprietary formats, and extensions
makes it applicable across many industries, but that flexibility introduces
interoperability challenges. Applications implement different subsets of USD
schemas, leading to inconsistencies when content moves between tools.

UsdProfiles addresses this by enabling applications to:

\li Discover and validate a document's compatibility and runtime requirements
\li Assess interoperability with particular scenes or scene elements
\li Determine capability satisfaction for declared functional needs

Rather than relying on ad hoc extension discovery, UsdProfiles introduces
a standardized, declarative approach.

\section usdProfiles_concepts Core Concepts

\subsection usdProfiles_capabilities Capabilities

A \b capability is an atomic unit of functionality, identified by a token
using reverse domain notation:

\li \c usd — base USD capability (root of the DAG)
\li \c usd.core — core USD interchange capabilities
\li \c com.nvidia.simready.geom — NVIDIA SimReady geometry capabilities
\li \c com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies — rigid body physics

Capabilities are organized in a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where edges
represent "depends on" relationships (called \b predecessors). Every
capability transitively depends on the \c usd root.

\code
usd (root)
├── usd.core
│   └── usd.core.v25_05  [PROFILE]
├── aousd.interchange
│   └── aousd.interchange.v1_0  [PROFILE]
└── com.nvidia.simready
    ├── com.nvidia.simready.geom
    ├── com.nvidia.simready.physics
    │   └── com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies
    └── com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral  [PROFILE]
\endcode

\subsection usdProfiles_profiles Profiles

A \b profile is a capability node tagged with \c "isProfile": \c true in the
capability DAG. It represents a coherent, named set of functionality suitable
for a specific use case. Examples:

\li \c usd.core.v25_05 — OpenUSD 25.05 baseline
\li \c aousd.interchange.v1_0 — AOUSD interchange requirements
\li \c com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral — neutral robotics prop for simulation

A profile's full capability set is determined by its transitive predecessors
in the DAG. For example, \c com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral
expands to:

\li \c com.nvidia.simready.geom
\li \c com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies
\li \c com.nvidia.simready.physics
\li \c com.nvidia.simready
\li \c usd

\subsection usdProfiles_naming Naming Convention

Capabilities use reverse domain notation for hierarchical clarity:

\li \b Canonical capabilities: \c usd.* — maintained by OpenUSD/AOUSD
\li \b Vendor extensions: \c com.nvidia.simready.* — NVIDIA SimReady
\li \b Custom extensions: \c com.example.myapp.* — your organization

All vendor capabilities must transitively inherit from \c usd. This ensures
that any asset's capability requirements can always be traced back to the
root, enabling clear compatibility reporting.

\section usdProfiles_schema ProfileAPI Schema

\subsection usdProfiles_schema_overview Overview

The \ref UsdProfilesProfileAPI "ProfileAPI" is a single-apply API schema
with two attributes:

\li \c profiles:profile (token) — the profile identifier this prim conforms to
\li \c profiles:capabilities (token[]) — additional explicit capability declarations

\subsection usdProfiles_authoring Authoring

\code{.py}
from pxr import Usd, UsdGeom, UsdProfiles

stage = Usd.Stage.CreateNew("my_asset.usda")
root = UsdGeom.Xform.Define(stage, "/MyRobot")

# Apply ProfileAPI and set a profile
api = UsdProfiles.ProfileAPI.Apply(root.GetPrim())
api.GetProfileAttr().Set("com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral")

# Optionally declare additional capabilities
api.CreateCapabilitiesAttr([
    "com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies",
    "com.nvidia.simready.geom",
])

stage.Save()
\endcode

The resulting USD file:

\code{.usda}
#usda 1.0

def Xform "MyRobot" (
    prepend apiSchemas = ["ProfileAPI"]
)
{
    token[] profiles:capabilities = [
        "com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies",
        "com.nvidia.simready.geom"
    ]
    token profiles:profile = "com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral"
}
\endcode

\subsection usdProfiles_resolution Resolution Semantics

ProfileAPI follows a \b "nearest \b ancestor \b wins" resolution rule:

\li If a prim has ProfileAPI applied with a non-empty \c profiles:profile,
    that profile governs the prim and its descendants.
\li If a prim does not have ProfileAPI, the nearest ancestor's profile applies.
\li Capability requirements do \b not propagate upward through hierarchies.

This enables strategic authoring at traversal boundaries for performance:

\code
/Warehouse                    ← ProfileAPI: "com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral"
  /Warehouse/ShelfUnit_A      ← (inherits from /Warehouse)
    /Warehouse/ShelfUnit_A/Frame  ← (inherits from /Warehouse)
  /Warehouse/RobotArm         ← ProfileAPI: "com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral"
    /Warehouse/RobotArm/Gripper   ← (inherits from /RobotArm)
\endcode

\code{.py}
# Query effective profile — walks ancestor chain
profile = UsdProfiles.GetEffectiveProfile(
    stage.GetPrimAtPath("/Warehouse/ShelfUnit_A/Frame"))
# → "com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral" (inherited from /Warehouse)
\endcode

\section usdProfiles_registry Capability Registry

\subsection usdProfiles_registry_overview Overview

The \ref UsdProfilesCapabilityRegistry "CapabilityRegistry" is a singleton
that collects capability declarations from all registered plugins at startup.
Capabilities are declared in \c plugInfo.json:

\code{.json}
{
    "Plugins": [{
        "Info": {
            "Capabilities": {
                "com.example.myCapability": {
                    "docstring": "Description of my capability",
                    "predecessors": ["usd"],
                    "isProfile": false
                },
                "com.example.myProfile": {
                    "docstring": "A profile combining capabilities",
                    "predecessors": [
                        "com.example.myCapability",
                        "com.nvidia.simready.geom"
                    ],
                    "isProfile": true
                }
            }
        }
    }]
}
\endcode

\subsection usdProfiles_registry_query Querying the Registry

\code{.py}
registry = UsdProfiles.CapabilityRegistry.GetInstance()

# Check membership
registry.IsCapability("com.nvidia.simready.geom")  # True
registry.IsProfile("com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral")  # True

# Navigate the DAG
registry.GetPredecessors("com.nvidia.simready.geom")
# → ["com.nvidia.simready"]

registry.GetTransitivePredecessors(
    "com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral")
# → ["com.nvidia.simready.geom",
#     "com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies",
#     "com.nvidia.simready.physics",
#     "com.nvidia.simready",
#     "usd"]

# List all
registry.GetAllCapabilities()  # all registered capability tokens
registry.GetAllProfiles()      # only those tagged as profiles
\endcode

\subsection usdProfiles_registry_extension Extending the Registry

Any USD plugin can declare additional capabilities in its \c plugInfo.json.
The registry loads from all plugins, so capabilities from multiple sources
are merged into a single DAG. This enables:

\li \b OpenUSD core to declare canonical \c usd.* capabilities
\li \b AOUSD to declare interchange \c aousd.* capabilities
\li \b Vendors to declare domain-specific capabilities (\c com.nvidia.simready.*)
\li \b Studios to declare project-specific capabilities (\c com.studio.project.*)

\section usdProfiles_profile_registry Profile Registry

The \ref UsdProfilesProfileRegistry "ProfileRegistry" is a convenience
layer that resolves profiles to their full capability sets:

\code{.py}
preg = UsdProfiles.ProfileRegistry.GetInstance()
preg.GetAllProfiles()
preg.GetProfileCapabilities("com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral")
# → ["com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral",
#     "com.nvidia.simready.geom",
#     "com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies",
#     "com.nvidia.simready.physics",
#     "com.nvidia.simready",
#     "usd"]
\endcode

\section usdProfiles_queries Convenience Query Functions

Two free functions provide the most common query patterns:

\li \ref UsdProfilesGetEffectiveProfile() — Walk the ancestor chain and
    return the nearest authored profile token.
\li \ref UsdProfilesGetEffectiveCapabilities() — Return the full capability
    set implied by the effective profile, plus any explicit
    \c profiles:capabilities on the authoring prim.

\code{.py}
# Get the profile governing this prim
profile = UsdProfiles.GetEffectiveProfile(prim)

# Get all capabilities this prim satisfies
caps = UsdProfiles.GetEffectiveCapabilities(prim)
\endcode

\section usdProfiles_example Full Example: Robotics Warehouse

\code{.py}
from pxr import Usd, UsdGeom, UsdProfiles

# Create a warehouse scene
stage = Usd.Stage.CreateNew("warehouse.usda")
stage.SetMetadata("upAxis", "Z")
stage.SetMetadata("metersPerUnit", 1.0)

# Warehouse root — declares SimReady profile
warehouse = UsdGeom.Xform.Define(stage, "/Warehouse")
api = UsdProfiles.ProfileAPI.Apply(warehouse.GetPrim())
api.GetProfileAttr().Set("com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral")

# Shelving (inherits profile from warehouse)
shelf = UsdGeom.Xform.Define(stage, "/Warehouse/ShelfUnit_A")
frame = UsdGeom.Xform.Define(stage, "/Warehouse/ShelfUnit_A/Frame")

# Robot arm (own profile with explicit capabilities)
robot = UsdGeom.Xform.Define(stage, "/Warehouse/RobotArm")
robot_api = UsdProfiles.ProfileAPI.Apply(robot.GetPrim())
robot_api.GetProfileAttr().Set(
    "com.nvidia.simready.prop_robotics_neutral")
robot_api.CreateCapabilitiesAttr([
    "com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies",
    "com.nvidia.simready.geom",
])
gripper = UsdGeom.Xform.Define(stage, "/Warehouse/RobotArm/Gripper")

stage.Save()

# --- Queries ---

# Effective profiles (nearest ancestor wins)
for path in ["/Warehouse", "/Warehouse/ShelfUnit_A/Frame",
             "/Warehouse/RobotArm/Gripper"]:
    prim = stage.GetPrimAtPath(path)
    profile = UsdProfiles.GetEffectiveProfile(prim)
    print(f"{path}: {profile}")

# Full capability set
caps = UsdProfiles.GetEffectiveCapabilities(
    stage.GetPrimAtPath("/Warehouse/RobotArm"))
print(f"Robot capabilities: {[str(c) for c in caps]}")

# Explore the registry
reg = UsdProfiles.CapabilityRegistry.GetInstance()
for cap in reg.GetAllProfiles():
    preds = reg.GetTransitivePredecessors(str(cap))
    print(f"Profile {cap} implies: {[str(p) for p in preds]}")
\endcode

\section usdProfiles_future Future Directions

The current implementation covers explicit capability queries via ProfileAPI.
Planned extensions include:

\li \b Introspective queries — infer capabilities from applied schemas
    (e.g. UsdPhysicsRigidBodyAPI implies \c com.nvidia.simready.physics.rigidBodies)
\li \b Traversal pruning — use ProfileAPI boundaries to optimize scene traversal
\li \b UsdValidation integration — select validators based on profile requirements
\li \b Build integration — guide OpenUSD builds based on capability profiles

